UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 4, Lesson 1: Plot twists and endings', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"A satisfying plot twist surprises readers but still fits earlier clues; endings should resolve the main problem without ignoring planted evidence.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same strategy to an unfamiliar topic or audience, then compare the effect of two different choices.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nWhich ending better fits a story where keys jingle in a coat all chapter: (a) keys were in the coat; (b) a spaceship took them with no earlier hint?\n\nFEEDBACK\n(a) fits planted sound clues; (b) ignores evidence and breaks trust.\n\nCHECK\nA twist that contradicts every earlier clue feels random, not clever.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nRead or invent a short story with one fair twist. List two earlier clues, explain why the twist fits and write an ending that solves the main problem.\n\nWrite a short reflection describing one specific revision you made and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"A satisfying plot twist surprises readers but still fits earlier clues; endings should resolve the main problem without ignoring planted evidence.","lesson_number":1,"lesson_role":"Reading","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["EN2-RECOM-01","EN2-UARL-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Read or invent a short story with one fair twist. List two earlier clues, explain why the twist fits and write an ending that solves the main problem. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using plot twist on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: A twist that contradicts every earlier clue feels random, not clever.. Correction: (a) fits planted sound clues;. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: A twist that contradicts every earlier clue feels random, not clever. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating plot twist on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with plot twist, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about plot twist with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for plot twist. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for plot twist. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","(a) fits planted sound clues","A twist that contradicts every earlier clue feels random, not clever"],"question":"Which ending better fits a story where keys jingle in a coat all chapter: (a) keys were in the coat; (b) a spaceship took them with no earlier hint?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for plot twist; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A satisfying plot twist surprises readers but still fits earlier clues","(a) fits planted sound clues","Careful use of ending and explain why it matters with clear evidence","A twist that contradicts every earlier clue feels random, not clever"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with plot twist?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named ending and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Apply plot twist to new material and name the ending and explain why it matters used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","A twist that contradicts every earlier clue feels random, not clever","Name ending and explain why it matters only, without linking it to plot twist"],"question":"Which response best applies plot twist with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The skill is shown by quoting or pointing to wording that carries plot twist. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A twist that contradicts every earlier clue feels random, not clever","Point to exact words in the sentence that show plot twist and say what they do","Ignore the sentence and invent a more exciting event","Say the writing is good without naming any words"],"question":"Read: \"Beside the oval, Kai practised bowling until the sun slipped low.\" Which choice best uses plot twist from this lesson?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the language skill with textual evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name ending and explain why it matters without linking it to any words in the text","(a) fits planted sound clues","A twist that contradicts every earlier clue