in the coat; (b) a spaceship took them with no earlier hint?\n\nFEEDBACK\n(a) fits planted sound clues; (b) ignores evidence and breaks trust.","explain":"A satisfying plot twist surprises readers but still fits earlier clues; endings should resolve the main problem without ignoring planted evidence.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"A twist that contradicts every earlier clue feels random, not clever. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Fair clue preparing a fitting twist.","caption":"Twists should use planted evidence.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Clue</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">muddy paw print</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Fitting twist</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">dog jumps low fence</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: Clue: a muddy paw print by the empty bird feeder. Expected: a cat. Twist that fits: the neighbour’s dog jumps the low fe Then write your own attempt.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: Clue: a muddy paw print by the empty bird feeder. Expected: a cat. Twist that fits: the neighbour’s dog jumps the low fe Then write your own attempt."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.","options":["A claim unrelated to the text","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no explanation","Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about plot twist with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nClue: a muddy paw print by the empty bird feeder. Expected: a cat. Twist that fits: the neighbour’s dog jumps the low fence at dawn. Ending: a taller latch stops the visits. The print prepared the twist. Think about how this word or relationship creates meaning.\n\nTRY FIRST\nUnderline or quote one precise detail in the model and explain its effect.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful evidence is a specific choice in the example, followed by an explanation of the effect on meaning.","explain":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather 